NIFT_TANSTACK_DOCUMENT_V1
{"document":{"type":"root","children":[{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"These are the public contracts from "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"@tanstack/ai-persistence"},{"type":"text","value":". Implement only the\nstores you need. Middleware turns behavior on from whichever stores are present, so\nthere is no separate enable list."}]},{"type":"table","align":[null,null,null],"header":[{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Store"}]},{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Purpose"}]},{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Used by"}]}],"rows":[[{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"inlineCode","value":"messages"}]},{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Authoritative model-message history per thread."}]},{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"inlineCode","value":"withPersistence"},{"type":"text","value":", required"}]}],[{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"inlineCode","value":"runs"}]},{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Run status, timing, errors, usage."}]},{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"inlineCode","value":"withPersistence"}]}],[{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"inlineCode","value":"interrupts"}]},{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Pending, resolved or cancelled human waits. Needs "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"runs"},{"type":"text","value":"."}]},{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"inlineCode","value":"withPersistence"}]}],[{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"inlineCode","value":"metadata"}]},{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"text","value":"App and integration key/value state."}]},{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"inlineCode","value":"withPersistence"}]}],[{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"inlineCode","value":"generationRuns"}]},{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Generation run status and result metadata, keyed by its own "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"runId"},{"type":"text","value":"."}]},{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"inlineCode","value":"withGenerationPersistence"},{"type":"text","value":", required"}]}],[{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"inlineCode","value":"artifacts"}]},{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"text","value":"File metadata. Needs "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"blobs"},{"type":"text","value":"."}]},{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"inlineCode","value":"withGenerationPersistence"},{"type":"text","value":", portable snapshots"}]}],[{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"inlineCode","value":"blobs"}]},{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"text","value":"File bytes. Needs "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"artifacts"},{"type":"text","value":"."}]},{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"inlineCode","value":"withGenerationPersistence"},{"type":"text","value":", portable snapshots"}]}]]},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Named groupings of the chat stores ("},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"ChatTranscriptStores"},{"type":"text","value":", "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"ChatPersistenceStores"},{"type":"text","value":",\n"},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"ChatWithInterruptsStores"},{"type":"text","value":") are covered in "},{"type":"link","href":"./controls","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Controls"}]},{"type":"text","value":"."}]},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"inlineCode","value":"runs"},{"type":"text","value":" is typed against "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"RunRecord"},{"type":"text","value":" and "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"RunStore"},{"type":"text","value":" from "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"@tanstack/ai"},{"type":"text","value":", not from this\npackage. One definition is what lets chat persistence and a sandbox run driver key on\nthe same record instead of disagreeing about one run."}]},{"type":"heading","depth":2,"id":"messagestore","children":[{"type":"text","value":"MessageStore"}]},{"type":"code","value":"import type { ModelMessage } from '@tanstack/ai'\n\ntype MessagePage =\n  | {\n      messages: Array<ModelMessage>\n      truncated: false\n      cursor?: never\n    }\n  | {\n      messages: Array<ModelMessage>\n      truncated: true\n      cursor: string\n    }\n\ninterface MessageStore {\n  loadThread(\n    threadId: string,\n    options?: { limit?: number; before?: string },\n  ): Promise<Array<ModelMessage> | MessagePage>\n  saveThread(threadId: string, messages: Array<ModelMessage>): Promise<void>\n}","lang":"ts"},{"type":"list","ordered":false,"items":[{"type":"listItem","children":[{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"inlineCode","value":"loadThread"},{"type":"text","value":" with only "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"threadId"},{"type":"text","value":" (middleware, "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"onStart"},{"type":"text","value":", "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"onFinish"},{"type":"text","value":") returns\nthe full array. Never a "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"MessagePage"},{"type":"text","value":"."}]}]},{"type":"listItem","children":[{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"inlineCode","value":"limit"},{"type":"text","value":" and "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"before"},{"type":"text","value":" are a paging hint for hydrate. You can ignore them and\nreturn the full array. "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"reconstructChat"},{"type":"text","value":" then slices after UI conversion."}]}]},{"type":"listItem","children":[{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"To page in the database, return a "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"MessagePage"},{"type":"text","value":". "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"truncated: true"},{"type":"text","value":" requires a\n"},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"cursor"},{"type":"text","value":". "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"truncated"},{"type":"text","value":" and "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"cursor"},{"type":"text","value":" use the same words as "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"BlobStore.list"},{"type":"text","value":"."}]}]},{"type":"listItem","children":[{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"If you ignore "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"before"},{"type":"text","value":" and return the newest array again, "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"reconstructChat"},{"type":"text","value":"\nloads the full thread and slices."}]}]},{"type":"listItem","children":[{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"inlineCode","value":"saveThread"},{"type":"text","value":" receives the full merged list. It is a replace, not an append.\nMerge by id is "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"withPersistence"},{"type":"text","value":", not this store."}]}]}]},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"inlineCode","value":"loadThread"},{"type":"text","value":" returns "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"[]"},{"type":"text","value":" (never "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"null"},{"type":"text","value":") for a thread that was never saved."}]},{"type":"heading","depth":2,"id":"runstore","children":[{"type":"text","value":"RunStore"}]},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"inlineCode","value":"RunStore"},{"type":"text","value":" and "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"RunRecord"},{"type":"text","value":" come from "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"@tanstack/ai"},{"type":"text","value":"; "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"@tanstack/ai-persistence"},{"type":"text","value":"\nre-exports both, which is why the samples on this page import them from either\npackage. "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"RunError"},{"type":"text","value":" is exported from "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"@tanstack/ai"},{"type":"text","value":" only."}]},{"type":"code","value":"import type { TokenUsage } from '@tanstack/ai'\n\ntype TerminalRunStatus = 'completed' | 'failed' | 'aborted'\ntype RunStatus = 'running' | 'interrupted' | TerminalRunStatus\n\n// Why a run failed. `message` is the provider's prose, which changes between\n// model versions and cannot be branched on; `code` is the stable\n// classification a consumer switches over to retry, escalate, or show a\n// specific UI. Providers do not always supply one, so `code` is optional.\ninterface RunError {\n  message: string\n  code?: string\n}\n\ninterface RunRecord {\n  runId: string\n  threadId: string\n  // Present on a subagent child run. Absent on the parent chat run.\n  parentRunId?: string\n  subagentRunId?: string\n  name?: string\n  status: RunStatus\n  startedAt: number // epoch ms\n  finishedAt?: number // epoch ms, set once the run reaches a terminal status\n  error?: RunError\n  usage?: TokenUsage // reported usage accumulated for this runId\n  // ---------------------------------------------------------------------------\n  // DURABLE SANDBOXED RUNS ONLY. A chat app never writes these four and nothing\n  // in `@tanstack/ai-persistence` reads them. Leave the columns out until you\n  // wire `withSandbox(sandbox, { runs, durability })`, and see\n  // [Build a Sandbox Adapter](./build-a-sandbox-adapter#the-four-run-fields)\n  // for what each one does and how to prove them.\n  // ---------------------------------------------------------------------------\n  sandboxKey?: string // which sandbox this run is bound to\n  detachedSince?: number // epoch ms since the last viewer left\n  cancelRequested?: boolean // an out-of-band cancel was recorded\n  driverEpoch?: number // fencing token, bumped by each host that claims the run\n}\n\ninterface RunStore {\n  // Required: insert-if-absent. An existing runId returns the stored record\n  // unchanged, so resuming a run never resets its startedAt or status.\n  createOrResume(input: {\n    runId: string\n    threadId: string\n    status?: RunStatus\n    startedAt: number\n    // Copied on the first insert only. A later call leaves them unchanged.\n    parentRunId?: string\n    subagentRunId?: string\n    name?: string\n  }): Promise<RunRecord>\n  // Required: patching an unknown runId is a no-op, not an error.\n  update(\n    runId: string,\n    patch: Partial<\n      Pick<\n        RunRecord,\n        | 'status'\n        | 'finishedAt'\n        | 'error'\n        | 'usage'\n        | 'sandboxKey'\n        | 'detachedSince'\n        | 'cancelRequested'\n        | 'driverEpoch'\n      >\n    >,\n  ): Promise<void>\n  // Required.\n  get(runId: string): Promise<RunRecord | null>\n  // Required. The most recent 'running' run for a thread (greatest\n  // `startedAt` wins), or null when the thread is idle. `reconstructChat`\n  // calls it to report `activeRun`, which is how a hydrating client tails a\n  // run that is still generating.\n  findActiveRun(threadId: string): Promise<RunRecord | null>\n  // Optional. Every run for a thread, ascending by startedAt. Only needed to\n  // render a thread's past agent activity.\n  listByThread?(threadId: string): Promise<Array<RunRecord>>\n  // Optional. Child runs for one parent, oldest startedAt first.\n  // reconstructChat uses this to put subagent cards back on the parent message.\n  listByParentRun?(parentRunId: string): Promise<Array<RunRecord>>\n  // Optional. Runs where status is 'running' and detachedSince <= now - ttlMs\n  // (inclusive). This is the query `reapDetachedRuns` (@tanstack/ai-sandbox)\n  // runs to find abandoned runs; scheduling that sweep is the app's job.\n  listReclaimable?(opts: {\n    now: number\n    ttlMs: number\n  }): Promise<Array<RunRecord>>\n}","lang":"ts"},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"inlineCode","value":"withPersistence"},{"type":"text","value":" sums reported numeric usage fields across provider calls for\nthe same "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"runId"},{"type":"text","value":". The opaque "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"providerUsageDetails"},{"type":"text","value":" field retains the latest\nreported bag. Known usage is persisted when the run interrupts or reaches a\nterminal status."}]},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"inlineCode","value":"createOrResume"},{"type":"text","value":", "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"update"},{"type":"text","value":", "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"get"},{"type":"text","value":", and "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"findActiveRun"},{"type":"text","value":" are the floor: a backend\nthat implements those four is a valid "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"RunStore"},{"type":"text","value":". Three contracts to hold:"}]},{"type":"list","ordered":false,"items":[{"type":"listItem","children":[{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"inlineCode","value":"createOrResume"},{"type":"text","value":" must be idempotent. A second call for an existing "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"runId"},{"type":"text","value":"\nreturns the complete stored record unchanged, including "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"usage"},{"type":"text","value":". This makes\nresuming a run safe and lets usage continue accumulating. Retries may repeat\nthe same run id."}]}]},{"type":"listItem","children":[{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"inlineCode","value":"update"},{"type":"text","value":" against an unknown "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"runId"},{"type":"text","value":" is a no-op."}]}]},{"type":"listItem","children":[{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"inlineCode","value":"findActiveRun"},{"type":"text","value":" must do real work. Stub it to "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"null"},{"type":"text","value":" and "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"reconstructChat"},{"type":"text","value":"\nalways reports "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"activeRun: null"},{"type":"text","value":", so a client that reloads (or switches back\nto) a still-generating thread restores the transcript but never resumes the\nlive reply. Nothing detects it either, because "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"null"},{"type":"text","value":" is also the right answer\nfor an idle thread. It was optional for exactly one release cycle and cost\nprecisely that, which is why it is required now."}]}]}]},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"inlineCode","value":"listByThread"},{"type":"text","value":", "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"listByParentRun"},{"type":"text","value":", and "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"listReclaimable"},{"type":"text","value":" are optional.\nConsumers feature-detect each one. After you declare an omission of\n"},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"listByThread"},{"type":"text","value":" or "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"listReclaimable"},{"type":"text","value":" in "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"skipMethods"},{"type":"text","value":", the conformance suite\nskips that method. Implement the ones\nyour app needs:"}]},{"type":"list","ordered":false,"items":[{"type":"listItem","children":[{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Skip "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"listByThread"},{"type":"text","value":" and a reload loses the cards of children that a tool call\nstarted. "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"reconstructChat"},{"type":"text","value":" calls it to find their parent runs."}]}]},{"type":"listItem","children":[{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Skip "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"listByParentRun"},{"type":"text","value":" and a reload shows the saved child text. The subagent\ncards stay absent. "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"reconstructChat"},{"type":"text","value":" calls this method to build the cards.\nThe conformance suite skips the subagent checks when this method is absent,\nso it needs no "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"skipMethods"},{"type":"text","value":" entry."}]}]},{"type":"listItem","children":[{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Skip "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"listReclaimable"},{"type":"text","value":" and the store cannot be reaped: "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"reapDetachedRuns"},{"type":"text","value":"\nfeature-detects it, logs one line, and sweeps nothing, so detached runs are\nnever finalized and their sandboxes never reclaimed. "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"detachedSince"},{"type":"text","value":" "},{"type":"emphasis","children":[{"type":"text","value":"is"}]},{"type":"text","value":"\npopulated for you by "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"withSandbox"},{"type":"text","value":"'s detach path, and note that the reaper is a\nfunction the application schedules, so implementing this method is necessary\nbut not by itself sufficient. See\n"},{"type":"link","href":"../sandbox/takeover#configuration","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Takeover & Detached Runs"}]},{"type":"text","value":" for the option,\nand "},{"type":"link","href":"../sandbox/reaping","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Reaping & Retention"}]},{"type":"text","value":" for the sweep itself and the\nschedules that drive it."}]}]}]},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"The four durable-run fields are a sandbox concern, so they are documented and\nproven on the sandbox side:\n"},{"type":"link","href":"./build-a-sandbox-adapter#the-four-run-fields","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Build a Sandbox Adapter"}]},{"type":"text","value":".\n"},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"runPersistenceConformance"},{"type":"text","value":" does not assert them, and a chat-only backend can leave\nthe columns out entirely. If you do wire durable sandboxed runs, run\n"},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"runDurableRunFieldsConformance"},{"type":"text","value":" from "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"@tanstack/ai-sandbox/testkit"},{"type":"text","value":" against the same\n"},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"runs"},{"type":"text","value":" store: each field breaks one mechanism silently when dropped, which is why it\ngets its own suite rather than a paragraph of warnings."}]},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Two helpers travel with the contract, and both exist so a caller does not have\nto reach for a cast:"}]},{"type":"list","ordered":false,"loose":true,"items":[{"type":"listItem","children":[{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Deciding \"can this run still emit events?\" from a bare "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"RunStatus"},{"type":"text","value":" string\nleaves you holding the wide union afterwards. "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"isTerminalRunStatus"},{"type":"text","value":" is a type\npredicate, so inside the guard the status is a "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"TerminalRunStatus"},{"type":"text","value":" and can be\npassed wherever one is required."}]},{"type":"code","value":"import { isTerminalRunStatus } from '@tanstack/ai-persistence'\nimport type { RunRecord, TerminalRunStatus } from '@tanstack/ai-persistence'\n\nfunction finalStatus(run: RunRecord): TerminalRunStatus | null {\n  return isTerminalRunStatus(run.status) ? run.status : null\n}","lang":"ts"}]},{"type":"listItem","children":[{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"An optional method you did implement should not need a "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"?."},{"type":"text","value":" from inside your\nown code. "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"defineRunStore"},{"type":"text","value":" is generic over the object you pass, so the result\nkeeps that object's exact shape: "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"createRunStore(db).findActiveRun(threadId)"},{"type":"text","value":"\nis a direct call on the store built in the\n"},{"type":"link","href":"./build-your-own-chat-adapter","children":[{"type":"text","value":"chat walkthrough"}]},{"type":"text","value":", while a consumer holding a\nbare "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"RunStore"},{"type":"text","value":" still feature-detects the same method."}]}]}]},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Capability tiers belong at the STORE level, not the method level. A backend that\ngenuinely has no run lifecycle should declare "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"ChatTranscriptStores"},{"type":"text","value":" and omit\n"},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"runs"},{"type":"text","value":" entirely rather than supply a "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"RunStore"},{"type":"text","value":" with a stubbed method: an absent\nstore is caught by the type system, an incomplete one fails silently at runtime."}]},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"inlineCode","value":"createOrResume"},{"type":"text","value":" copies "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"parentRunId"},{"type":"text","value":", "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"subagentRunId"},{"type":"text","value":", and "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"name"},{"type":"text","value":" on the first\ninsert. A second call for the same "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"runId"},{"type":"text","value":" leaves those fields unchanged. If\nthe caller omits a field, omit that field on the stored record. Do not write\n"},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"''"},{"type":"text","value":" for a field the caller did not pass."}]},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"The reference implementation, "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"MemoryRunStore"},{"type":"text","value":" in\n"},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"packages/ai-persistence/src/memory.ts"},{"type":"text","value":", implements all seven run methods. The\n"},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"examples/ts-react-chat"},{"type":"text","value":" SQLite adapter ("},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"src/lib/sqlite-persistence.ts"},{"type":"text","value":")\nimplements all seven run methods."}]},{"type":"heading","depth":2,"id":"interruptstore","children":[{"type":"text","value":"InterruptStore"}]},{"type":"code","value":"interface InterruptRecord {\n  interruptId: string\n  runId: string\n  threadId: string\n  status: 'pending' | 'resolved' | 'cancelled'\n  requestedAt: number // epoch ms\n  resolvedAt?: number // epoch ms, set once resolved or cancelled\n  payload: Record<string, unknown>\n  response?: unknown\n}\n\ntype InterruptCommitEntry =\n  | { interruptId: string; status: 'resolved'; response?: unknown }\n  | { interruptId: string; status: 'cancelled' }\n\ninterface InterruptStore {\n  create(record: Omit<InterruptRecord, 'status' | 'resolvedAt'>): Promise<void>\n  commitBatch?(entries: ReadonlyArray<InterruptCommitEntry>): Promise<void>\n  resolve(interruptId: string, response?: unknown): Promise<void>\n  cancel(interruptId: string): Promise<void>\n  get(interruptId: string): Promise<InterruptRecord | null>\n  list(threadId: string): Promise<Array<InterruptRecord>>\n  listPending(threadId: string): Promise<Array<InterruptRecord>>\n  listByRun(runId: string): Promise<Array<InterruptRecord>>\n  listPendingByRun(runId: string): Promise<Array<InterruptRecord>>\n}","lang":"ts"},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"inlineCode","value":"commitBatch"},{"type":"text","value":" is optional. Use one database transaction for all entries when\nyou implement it. The legacy "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"resolve"},{"type":"text","value":" and "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"cancel"},{"type":"text","value":" fallback is sequential and\nis not atomic."}]},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"If you implement "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"commitBatch"},{"type":"text","value":", reject the whole batch (throw, write nothing)\nwhen any entry has a duplicate "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"interruptId"},{"type":"text","value":", names an interrupt that does not\nexist, or names an interrupt that is not "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"'pending'"},{"type":"text","value":". "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"resolve"},{"type":"text","value":" and "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"cancel"},{"type":"text","value":"\nstay no-ops for a missing "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"interruptId"},{"type":"text","value":"."}]},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"inlineCode","value":"create"},{"type":"text","value":" accepts a record without "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"status"},{"type":"text","value":"/"},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"resolvedAt"},{"type":"text","value":" so every interrupt is\nborn "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"'pending'"},{"type":"text","value":"; it is insert-if-absent, so a duplicate "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"create"},{"type":"text","value":" never clobbers\nan already-resolved interrupt. The "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"list*"},{"type":"text","value":" methods return records ordered by\n"},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"requestedAt"},{"type":"text","value":" ascending. An "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"interrupts"},{"type":"text","value":" store requires a "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"runs"},{"type":"text","value":" store when used\nwith chat persistence."}]},{"type":"heading","depth":2,"id":"metadatastore","children":[{"type":"text","value":"MetadataStore"}]},{"type":"code","value":"interface MetadataStore {\n  get(scope: string, key: string): Promise<unknown | null>\n  set(scope: string, key: string, value: unknown): Promise<void>\n  delete(scope: string, key: string): Promise<void>\n}","lang":"ts"},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Namespaces and value schemas are application-owned, and "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"(scope, key)"},{"type":"text","value":" is the\ncomposite identity. A stored "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"null"},{"type":"text","value":" is indistinguishable from absence at the type\nlevel, so wrap a value you must persist as "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"null"},{"type":"text","value":" (e.g. "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"{ value: null }"},{"type":"text","value":"), or\nreject nullish values outright the way the SQLite store above does."}]},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"inlineCode","value":"withPersistence"},{"type":"text","value":" also provides this store through the core\n"},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"MetadataCapability"},{"type":"text","value":". Middleware can use it for derived state without depending\non "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"@tanstack/ai-persistence"},{"type":"text","value":". For example, "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"withCompaction"},{"type":"text","value":" stores validated\ncontext checkpoints here. Do not place the canonical transcript in metadata;\nthe "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"messages"},{"type":"text","value":" store owns it."}]},{"type":"heading","depth":2,"id":"generationrunstore","children":[{"type":"text","value":"GenerationRunStore"}]},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"The generation counterpart to "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"RunStore"},{"type":"text","value":". Keyed by its own "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"runId"},{"type":"text","value":", with\n"},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"threadId"},{"type":"text","value":" the slot "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"findLatestForThread"},{"type":"text","value":" looks runs up by.\n"},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"withGenerationPersistence"},{"type":"text","value":" requires this store, not "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"runs"},{"type":"text","value":"."}]},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Its "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"status"},{"type":"text","value":" uses the same vocabulary as a chat run's "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"RunStatus"},{"type":"text","value":", so one status\ncolumn and one set of checks cover both tables."}]},{"type":"code","value":"import type { PersistedArtifactRef, TokenUsage } from '@tanstack/ai'\n\n// The same vocabulary as a chat run's `RunStatus`.\ntype GenerationRunStatus =\n  | 'running'\n  | 'interrupted'\n  | 'completed'\n  | 'failed'\n  | 'aborted'\n\ninterface GenerationRunRecord {\n  runId: string\n  threadId: string // the slot this run fills, hydrated by findLatestForThread\n  activity: string // 'image' | 'audio' | 'tts' | 'video' | 'transcription'\n  provider: string\n  model: string\n  status: GenerationRunStatus\n  startedAt: number // epoch ms\n  finishedAt?: number // epoch ms, set once the run reaches a terminal status\n  error?: { message: string; code?: string }\n  result?: unknown // terminal result metadata (ids, urls), never media bytes\n  artifacts?: Array<PersistedArtifactRef> // present with an artifacts + blobs backend\n  usage?: TokenUsage\n}\n\ninterface GenerationRunStore {\n  createOrResume(input: {\n    runId: string\n    activity: string\n    provider: string\n    model: string\n    startedAt: number\n    threadId: string\n    status?: GenerationRunStatus\n  }): Promise<GenerationRunRecord>\n  update(\n    runId: string,\n    patch: Partial<\n      Pick<\n        GenerationRunRecord,\n        'status' | 'finishedAt' | 'error' | 'result' | 'artifacts' | 'usage'\n      >\n    >,\n  ): Promise<void>\n  get(runId: string): Promise<GenerationRunRecord | null>\n  // The most recent run filed under a thread (greatest `startedAt`), or null.\n  // Required: it is the only query that hydrates a generation, so an adapter\n  // without it would be indistinguishable from one whose thread has no runs:\n  // `persistence: true` would silently restore nothing, forever.\n  findLatestForThread(threadId: string): Promise<GenerationRunRecord | null>\n}","lang":"ts"},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Implement "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"createOrResume"},{"type":"text","value":" idempotently: a second call for an existing "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"runId"},{"type":"text","value":"\nreturns the stored record unchanged ("},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"startedAt"},{"type":"text","value":" / "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"activity"},{"type":"text","value":" / "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"provider"},{"type":"text","value":" /\n"},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"model"},{"type":"text","value":" / "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"threadId"},{"type":"text","value":" are not mutated), which is what makes resuming a run safe.\n"},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"update"},{"type":"text","value":" against an unknown "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"runId"},{"type":"text","value":" is a no-op."}]},{"type":"heading","depth":2,"id":"artifactstore","children":[{"type":"text","value":"ArtifactStore"}]},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Metadata rows for persisted media. The bytes live in a "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"BlobStore"},{"type":"text","value":"; this record\nholds the descriptive metadata and an optional "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"sourceUrl"},{"type":"text","value":" for reference-only\nbackends. Provide it together with a "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"BlobStore"},{"type":"text","value":" to keep generated bytes."}]},{"type":"code","value":"interface ArtifactRecord {\n  artifactId: string\n  runId: string\n  threadId: string\n  blobKey?: string // where the bytes live; absent on pre-blobKey records\n  name: string\n  mimeType: string\n  size: number\n  sourceUrl?: string // where the bytes were fetched FROM (provenance)\n  createdAt: number // epoch ms\n}\n\ninterface ArtifactStore {\n  save: (record: ArtifactRecord) => Promise<void>\n  get: (artifactId: string) => Promise<ArtifactRecord | null>\n  list: (runId: string) => Promise<Array<ArtifactRecord>> // [] when the run has none\n  // Complete thread history, ordered by (createdAt, artifactId) ascending.\n  listForThread: (threadId: string) => Promise<Array<ArtifactRecord>>\n  delete: (artifactId: string) => Promise<void>\n  deleteForRun: (runId: string) => Promise<void>\n}","lang":"ts"},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"inlineCode","value":"list"},{"type":"text","value":" and "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"listForThread"},{"type":"text","value":" use "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"createdAt"},{"type":"text","value":" first, then ordinal bytewise\n"},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"artifactId"},{"type":"text","value":" order. Compare UTF-8 bytes from left to right. Do not use locale\ncollation."}]},{"type":"heading","depth":2,"id":"blobstore","children":[{"type":"text","value":"BlobStore"}]},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"A durable object/blob store for the bytes. "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"withGenerationPersistence"},{"type":"text","value":" writes\neach generated file under the key "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"artifacts/<runId>/<artifactId>"},{"type":"text","value":"."}]},{"type":"code","value":"type BlobBody =\n  | ReadableStream<Uint8Array>\n  | ArrayBuffer\n  | ArrayBufferView\n  | string\n  | Blob\n\ninterface BlobRecord {\n  key: string\n  size?: number\n  etag?: string\n  contentType?: string\n  customMetadata?: Record<string, string>\n  createdAt?: number // epoch ms first written\n  updatedAt?: number // epoch ms last overwritten\n}\n\ninterface BlobObject extends BlobRecord {\n  arrayBuffer(): Promise<ArrayBuffer>\n  text(): Promise<string>\n  body?: ReadableStream<Uint8Array>\n  // The slice served, when a range was requested. Absent on a whole read.\n  range?: { offset: number; length: number }\n}\n\ninterface BlobListPage {\n  objects: Array<BlobRecord>\n  cursor?: string // present only when `truncated`\n  truncated?: boolean\n}\n\ninterface BlobPutOptions {\n  contentType?: string\n  customMetadata?: Record<string, string>\n  // Exact byte length of `body`, when the producer knows it. Advisory: use it\n  // to pick an upload strategy (single-shot vs multipart), never as a\n  // substitute for counting the bytes you actually store.\n  expectedLength?: number\n}\n\ninterface BlobRange {\n  offset: number // from the start of the object; must be inside it\n  length?: number // defaults to \"to the end\"; clamped when it overshoots\n}\n\ninterface BlobGetOptions {\n  range?: BlobRange\n}\n\ninterface BlobListOptions {\n  prefix?: string\n  cursor?: string\n  limit?: number\n}\n\ninterface BlobStore {\n  put(key: string, body: BlobBody, options?: BlobPutOptions): Promise<BlobRecord>\n  get(key: string, options?: BlobGetOptions): Promise<BlobObject | null>\n  head(key: string): Promise<BlobRecord | null>\n  delete(key: string): Promise<void>\n  list(options?: BlobListOptions): Promise<BlobListPage>\n}","lang":"ts"},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Three contracts to hold for "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"list"},{"type":"text","value":":"}]},{"type":"list","ordered":false,"items":[{"type":"listItem","children":[{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"inlineCode","value":"prefix"},{"type":"text","value":" matches literally and case-sensitively. Escape SQL "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"LIKE"},{"type":"text","value":"\nmetacharacters."}]}]},{"type":"listItem","children":[{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"When "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"limit"},{"type":"text","value":" is given and more keys match, return "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"truncated: true"},{"type":"text","value":" with a\n"},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"cursor"},{"type":"text","value":". Passing that cursor back returns the strictly-following keys, so\npaging visits every key exactly once."}]}]},{"type":"listItem","children":[{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"inlineCode","value":"limit: 0"},{"type":"text","value":" yields an empty, untruncated page."}]}]}]},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"And one for "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"put"},{"type":"text","value":": the body can be a "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"ReadableStream"},{"type":"text","value":" with "},{"type":"strong","children":[{"type":"text","value":"no declared\nlength"}]},{"type":"text","value":". That is how a URL-fetched artifact arrives whenever the origin does\nnot declare one it can be held to: a chunked reply, or a compressed one whose\n"},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"content-length"},{"type":"text","value":" describes the compressed bytes. (When the origin "},{"type":"emphasis","children":[{"type":"text","value":"does"}]},{"type":"text","value":" declare\na usable length, the body reaches you exactly as "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"fetch"},{"type":"text","value":" produced it, length\nintact, and "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"expectedLength"},{"type":"text","value":" carries the same number.) Your store must drain a\nlength-less stream, not require a length up front. Backends that need a declared\nlength for a single-shot upload (Cloudflare R2 on workerd is one) can re-attach\n"},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"expectedLength"},{"type":"text","value":" when it is present and stream through a multipart upload when it\nis not; the "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"ai-persistence/build-cloudflare-artifact-store"},{"type":"text","value":" skill ships that\nrecipe. The conformance testkit exercises the length-less case, so a store that\nonly handles byte bodies fails the suite."}]},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"And one for "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"get"},{"type":"text","value":": honour "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"options.range"},{"type":"text","value":" by returning "},{"type":"strong","children":[{"type":"text","value":"only that slice"}]},{"type":"text","value":".\n"},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"size"},{"type":"text","value":" keeps reporting the whole object, and the returned "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"range"},{"type":"text","value":" reports what\nyou actually served. Together they are the "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"206"},{"type":"text","value":" response a media player's\nseeking depends on. "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"resolveBlobRange(size, range)"},{"type":"text","value":" does the clamping (a\n"},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"length"},{"type":"text","value":" past the end is legal and clamps; an "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"offset"},{"type":"text","value":" past the end throws,\nbecause a serve route should have answered "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"416"},{"type":"text","value":" from "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"record.size"},{"type":"text","value":" first):"}]},{"type":"code","value":"import { resolveBlobRange } from '@tanstack/ai-persistence'\n\nasync get(key: string, options?: BlobGetOptions) {\n  const row = await selectBlob(key)\n  if (!row) return null\n  if (!options?.range) return blobObject(row, row.body)\n  const served = resolveBlobRange(row.size, options.range)\n  // Slice at the storage layer, not after loading the whole object.\n  const bytes = await selectBlobSlice(key, served.offset, served.length)\n  return blobObject(row, bytes, served)\n}","lang":"ts","meta":"ignore"},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"This is not optional for a store that holds bytes, and the conformance testkit\nasserts it. Ignoring "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"range"},{"type":"text","value":" and returning the whole file is what makes\n"},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"<video>"},{"type":"text","value":" seeking (and Safari playback at all) fail, and it silently sends the\nentire artifact for every seek. A reference-only backend that stores no bytes\nskips "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"blobs"},{"type":"text","value":" altogether instead."}]},{"type":"heading","depth":2,"id":"how-the-records-relate","children":[{"type":"text","value":"How the records relate"}]},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"The thread is not a table of its own: it exists as the "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"thread_id"},{"type":"text","value":" the other records\nhang off. "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"metadata"},{"type":"text","value":" stands apart, keyed by "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"(namespace, key)"},{"type":"text","value":". Note the asymmetry on\nthe generation side, where a run is keyed by its own "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"run_id"},{"type":"text","value":" first and its\n"},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"thread_id"},{"type":"text","value":" names the slot the run fills, which is what "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"findLatestForThread"},{"type":"text","value":"\nhydrates by."}]},{"type":"code","value":"erDiagram\n    MESSAGES ||--o{ RUN : \"thread_id, a thread has many runs\"\n    RUN ||--o{ INTERRUPT : \"run_id, a run may pause on interrupts\"\n    MESSAGES ||..o{ GENERATION_RUN : \"thread_id, the slot a run fills\"\n    GENERATION_RUN ||--o{ ARTIFACT : \"run_id, a run produces artifacts\"\n    ARTIFACT ||--|| BLOB : \"blob_key, the bytes\"\n\n    MESSAGES {\n        string thread_id PK\n        json messages_json \"full transcript, overwritten on save\"\n    }\n    RUN {\n        string run_id PK\n        string thread_id\n        string status \"running | completed | failed | interrupted\"\n        int started_at\n        int finished_at\n    }\n    INTERRUPT {\n        string interrupt_id PK\n        string run_id\n        string thread_id\n        string status \"pending | resolved | cancelled\"\n        int requested_at\n    }\n    GENERATION_RUN {\n        string run_id PK\n        string thread_id \"the slot this run fills\"\n        string activity \"image | audio | tts | video | transcription\"\n        string status \"running | completed | failed | interrupted\"\n    }\n    ARTIFACT {\n        string artifact_id PK\n        string run_id\n        string blob_key \"where the bytes live\"\n        string mime_type\n        int size\n    }\n    BLOB {\n        string key PK\n        blob bytes\n    }","lang":"mermaid"},{"type":"heading","depth":2,"id":"typing-an-adapter","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Typing an adapter"}]},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Each store has a "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"define*Store"},{"type":"text","value":" helper ("},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"defineMessageStore"},{"type":"text","value":", "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"defineRunStore"},{"type":"text","value":",\n"},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"defineInterruptStore"},{"type":"text","value":", "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"defineMetadataStore"},{"type":"text","value":", "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"defineGenerationRunStore"},{"type":"text","value":",\n"},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"defineArtifactStore"},{"type":"text","value":", "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"defineBlobStore"},{"type":"text","value":"). They compose into "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"defineAIPersistence"},{"type":"text","value":",\nwhich tracks exact presence: the stores you passed autocomplete on\n"},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"persistence.stores"},{"type":"text","value":", and reading one you did not pass is a compile error."}]},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Those seven keys are the only ones "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"stores"},{"type":"text","value":" accepts. Anything else throws\n"},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"Unknown AIPersistence store key"},{"type":"text","value":" at construction."}]},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"To annotate the value instead, use a named shape:"}]},{"type":"list","ordered":false,"items":[{"type":"listItem","children":[{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"inlineCode","value":"ChatPersistence"},{"type":"text","value":": all four chat stores."}]}]},{"type":"listItem","children":[{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"inlineCode","value":"ChatTranscriptPersistence"},{"type":"text","value":": the "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"messages"},{"type":"text","value":" floor."}]}]},{"type":"listItem","children":[{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"inlineCode","value":"AIPersistence"},{"type":"text","value":": the all-optional bag. "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"withPersistence"},{"type":"text","value":" rejects it, because\n"},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"stores.messages"},{"type":"text","value":" is possibly "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"undefined"},{"type":"text","value":"."}]}]}]},{"type":"heading","depth":2,"id":"where-to-go-next","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Where to go next"}]},{"type":"list","ordered":false,"items":[{"type":"listItem","children":[{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"link","href":"./build-your-own-chat-adapter","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Build a chat adapter"}]},{"type":"text","value":": these contracts implemented against SQLite."}]}]},{"type":"listItem","children":[{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"link","href":"./build-your-own-generation-adapter","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Build a generation adapter"}]},{"type":"text","value":": the generation half."}]}]},{"type":"listItem","children":[{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"link","href":"./build-your-own-adapter#verify-with-the-conformance-suite","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Build your own adapter"}]},{"type":"text","value":": check an implementation against the conformance suite."}]}]}]}],"headings":[{"id":"messagestore","text":"MessageStore","level":2},{"id":"runstore","text":"RunStore","level":2},{"id":"interruptstore","text":"InterruptStore","level":2},{"id":"metadatastore","text":"MetadataStore","level":2},{"id":"generationrunstore","text":"GenerationRunStore","level":2},{"id":"artifactstore","text":"ArtifactStore","level":2},{"id":"blobstore","text":"BlobStore","level":2},{"id":"how-the-records-relate","text":"How the records relate","level":2},{"id":"typing-an-adapter","text":"Typing an adapter","level":2},{"id":"where-to-go-next","text":"Where to go next","level":2}]},"frameworks":[],"downloadMarkdown":"\nThese are the public contracts from `@tanstack/ai-persistence`. Implement only the\nstores you need. Middleware turns behavior on from whichever stores are present, so\nthere is no separate enable list.\n\n| Store | Purpose | Used by |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| `messages` | Authoritative model-message history per thread. | `withPersistence`, required |\n| `runs` | Run status, timing, errors, usage. | `withPersistence` |\n| `interrupts` | Pending, resolved or cancelled human waits. Needs `runs`. | `withPersistence` |\n| `metadata` | App and integration key/value state. | `withPersistence` |\n| `generationRuns` | Generation run status and result metadata, keyed by its own `runId`. | `withGenerationPersistence`, required |\n| `artifacts` | File metadata. Needs `blobs`. | `withGenerationPersistence`, portable snapshots |\n| `blobs` | File bytes. Needs `artifacts`. | `withGenerationPersistence`, portable snapshots |\n\nNamed groupings of the chat stores (`ChatTranscriptStores`, `ChatPersistenceStores`,\n`ChatWithInterruptsStores`) are covered in [Controls](./controls).\n\n`runs` is typed against `RunRecord` and `RunStore` from `@tanstack/ai`, not from this\npackage. One definition is what lets chat persistence and a sandbox run driver key on\nthe same record instead of disagreeing about one run.\n\n## MessageStore\n\n```ts\nimport type { ModelMessage } from '@tanstack/ai'\n\ntype MessagePage =\n  | {\n      messages: Array<ModelMessage>\n      truncated: false\n      cursor?: never\n    }\n  | {\n      messages: Array<ModelMessage>\n      truncated: true\n      cursor: string\n    }\n\ninterface MessageStore {\n  loadThread(\n    threadId: string,\n    options?: { limit?: number; before?: string },\n  ): Promise<Array<ModelMessage> | MessagePage>\n  saveThread(threadId: string, messages: Array<ModelMessage>): Promise<void>\n}\n```\n\n- `loadThread` with only `threadId` (middleware, `onStart`, `onFinish`) returns\n  the full array. Never a `MessagePage`.\n- `limit` and `before` are a paging hint for hydrate. You can ignore them and\n  return the full array. `reconstructChat` then slices after UI conversion.\n- To page in the database, return a `MessagePage`. `truncated: true` requires a\n  `cursor`. `truncated` and `cursor` use the same words as `BlobStore.list`.\n- If you ignore `before` and return the newest array again, `reconstructChat`\n  loads the full thread and slices.\n- `saveThread` receives the full merged list. It is a replace, not an append.\n  Merge by id is `withPersistence`, not this store.\n\n`loadThread` returns `[]` (never `null`) for a thread that was never saved.\n\n## RunStore\n\n`RunStore` and `RunRecord` come from `@tanstack/ai`; `@tanstack/ai-persistence`\nre-exports both, which is why the samples on this page import them from either\npackage. `RunError` is exported from `@tanstack/ai` only.\n\n```ts\nimport type { TokenUsage } from '@tanstack/ai'\n\ntype TerminalRunStatus = 'completed' | 'failed' | 'aborted'\ntype RunStatus = 'running' | 'interrupted' | TerminalRunStatus\n\n// Why a run failed. `message` is the provider's prose, which changes between\n// model versions and cannot be branched on; `code` is the stable\n// classification a consumer switches over to retry, escalate, or show a\n// specific UI. Providers do not always supply one, so `code` is optional.\ninterface RunError {\n  message: string\n  code?: string\n}\n\ninterface RunRecord {\n  runId: string\n  threadId: string\n  // Present on a subagent child run. Absent on the parent chat run.\n  parentRunId?: string\n  subagentRunId?: string\n  name?: string\n  status: RunStatus\n  startedAt: number // epoch ms\n  finishedAt?: number // epoch ms, set once the run reaches a terminal status\n  error?: RunError\n  usage?: TokenUsage // reported usage accumulated for this runId\n  // ---------------------------------------------------------------------------\n  // DURABLE SANDBOXED RUNS ONLY. A chat app never writes these four and nothing\n  // in `@tanstack/ai-persistence` reads them. Leave the columns out until you\n  // wire `withSandbox(sandbox, { runs, durability })`, and see\n  // [Build a Sandbox Adapter](./build-a-sandbox-adapter#the-four-run-fields)\n  // for what each one does and how to prove them.\n  // ---------------------------------------------------------------------------\n  sandboxKey?: string // which sandbox this run is bound to\n  detachedSince?: number // epoch ms since the last viewer left\n  cancelRequested?: boolean // an out-of-band cancel was recorded\n  driverEpoch?: number // fencing token, bumped by each host that claims the run\n}\n\ninterface RunStore {\n  // Required: insert-if-absent. An existing runId returns the stored record\n  // unchanged, so resuming a run never resets its startedAt or status.\n  createOrResume(input: {\n    runId: string\n    threadId: string\n    status?: RunStatus\n    startedAt: number\n    // Copied on the first insert only. A later call leaves them unchanged.\n    parentRunId?: string\n    subagentRunId?: string\n    name?: string\n  }): Promise<RunRecord>\n  // Required: patching an unknown runId is a no-op, not an error.\n  update(\n    runId: string,\n    patch: Partial<\n      Pick<\n        RunRecord,\n        | 'status'\n        | 'finishedAt'\n        | 'error'\n        | 'usage'\n        | 'sandboxKey'\n        | 'detachedSince'\n        | 'cancelRequested'\n        | 'driverEpoch'\n      >\n    >,\n  ): Promise<void>\n  // Required.\n  get(runId: string): Promise<RunRecord | null>\n  // Required. The most recent 'running' run for a thread (greatest\n  // `startedAt` wins), or null when the thread is idle. `reconstructChat`\n  // calls it to report `activeRun`, which is how a hydrating client tails a\n  // run that is still generating.\n  findActiveRun(threadId: string): Promise<RunRecord | null>\n  // Optional. Every run for a thread, ascending by startedAt. Only needed to\n  // render a thread's past agent activity.\n  listByThread?(threadId: string): Promise<Array<RunRecord>>\n  // Optional. Child runs for one parent, oldest startedAt first.\n  // reconstructChat uses this to put subagent cards back on the parent message.\n  listByParentRun?(parentRunId: string): Promise<Array<RunRecord>>\n  // Optional. Runs where status is 'running' and detachedSince <= now - ttlMs\n  // (inclusive). This is the query `reapDetachedRuns` (@tanstack/ai-sandbox)\n  // runs to find abandoned runs; scheduling that sweep is the app's job.\n  listReclaimable?(opts: {\n    now: number\n    ttlMs: number\n  }): Promise<Array<RunRecord>>\n}\n```\n\n`withPersistence` sums reported numeric usage fields across provider calls for\nthe same `runId`. The opaque `providerUsageDetails` field retains the latest\nreported bag. Known usage is persisted when the run interrupts or reaches a\nterminal status.\n\n`createOrResume`, `update`, `get`, and `findActiveRun` are the floor: a backend\nthat implements those four is a valid `RunStore`. Three contracts to hold:\n\n- `createOrResume` must be idempotent. A second call for an existing `runId`\n  returns the complete stored record unchanged, including `usage`. This makes\n  resuming a run safe and lets usage continue accumulating. Retries may repeat\n  the same run id.\n- `update` against an unknown `runId` is a no-op.\n- `findActiveRun` must do real work. Stub it to `null` and `reconstructChat`\n  always reports `activeRun: null`, so a client that reloads (or switches back\n  to) a still-generating thread restores the transcript but never resumes the\n  live reply. Nothing detects it either, because `null` is also the right answer\n  for an idle thread. It was optional for exactly one release cycle and cost\n  precisely that, which is why it is required now.\n\n`listByThread`, `listByParentRun`, and `listReclaimable` are optional.\nConsumers feature-detect each one. After you declare an omission of\n`listByThread` or `listReclaimable` in `skipMethods`, the conformance suite\nskips that method. Implement the ones\nyour app needs:\n\n- Skip `listByThread` and a reload loses the cards of children that a tool call\n  started. `reconstructChat` calls it to find their parent runs.\n- Skip `listByParentRun` and a reload shows the saved child text. The subagent\n  cards stay absent. `reconstructChat` calls this method to build the cards.\n  The conformance suite skips the subagent checks when this method is absent,\n  so it needs no `skipMethods` entry.\n- Skip `listReclaimable` and the store cannot be reaped: `reapDetachedRuns`\n  feature-detects it, logs one line, and sweeps nothing, so detached runs are\n  never finalized and their sandboxes never reclaimed. `detachedSince` *is*\n  populated for you by `withSandbox`'s detach path, and note that the reaper is a\n  function the application schedules, so implementing this method is necessary\n  but not by itself sufficient. See\n  [Takeover & Detached Runs](../sandbox/takeover#configuration) for the option,\n  and [Reaping & Retention](../sandbox/reaping) for the sweep itself and the\n  schedules that drive it.\n\nThe four durable-run fields are a sandbox concern, so they are documented and\nproven on the sandbox side:\n[Build a Sandbox Adapter](./build-a-sandbox-adapter#the-four-run-fields).\n`runPersistenceConformance` does not assert them, and a chat-only backend can leave\nthe columns out entirely. If you do wire durable sandboxed runs, run\n`runDurableRunFieldsConformance` from `@tanstack/ai-sandbox/testkit` against the same\n`runs` store: each field breaks one mechanism silently when dropped, which is why it\ngets its own suite rather than a paragraph of warnings.\n\nTwo helpers travel with the contract, and both exist so a caller does not have\nto reach for a cast:\n\n- Deciding \"can this run still emit events?\" from a bare `RunStatus` string\n  leaves you holding the wide union afterwards. `isTerminalRunStatus` is a type\n  predicate, so inside the guard the status is a `TerminalRunStatus` and can be\n  passed wherever one is required.\n\n  ```ts\n  import { isTerminalRunStatus } from '@tanstack/ai-persistence'\n  import type { RunRecord, TerminalRunStatus } from '@tanstack/ai-persistence'\n\n  function finalStatus(run: RunRecord): TerminalRunStatus | null {\n    return isTerminalRunStatus(run.status) ? run.status : null\n  }\n  ```\n\n- An optional method you did implement should not need a `?.` from inside your\n  own code. `defineRunStore` is generic over the object you pass, so the result\n  keeps that object's exact shape: `createRunStore(db).findActiveRun(threadId)`\n  is a direct call on the store built in the\n  [chat walkthrough](./build-your-own-chat-adapter), while a consumer holding a\n  bare `RunStore` still feature-detects the same method.\n\nCapability tiers belong at the STORE level, not the method level. A backend that\ngenuinely has no run lifecycle should declare `ChatTranscriptStores` and omit\n`runs` entirely rather than supply a `RunStore` with a stubbed method: an absent\nstore is caught by the type system, an incomplete one fails silently at runtime.\n\n`createOrResume` copies `parentRunId`, `subagentRunId`, and `name` on the first\ninsert. A second call for the same `runId` leaves those fields unchanged. If\nthe caller omits a field, omit that field on the stored record. Do not write\n`''` for a field the caller did not pass.\n\nThe reference implementation, `MemoryRunStore` in\n`packages/ai-persistence/src/memory.ts`, implements all seven run methods. The\n`examples/ts-react-chat` SQLite adapter (`src/lib/sqlite-persistence.ts`)\nimplements all seven run methods.\n\n## InterruptStore\n\n```ts\ninterface InterruptRecord {\n  interruptId: string\n  runId: string\n  threadId: string\n  status: 'pending' | 'resolved' | 'cancelled'\n  requestedAt: number // epoch ms\n  resolvedAt?: number // epoch ms, set once resolved or cancelled\n  payload: Record<string, unknown>\n  response?: unknown\n}\n\ntype InterruptCommitEntry =\n  | { interruptId: string; status: 'resolved'; response?: unknown }\n  | { interruptId: string; status: 'cancelled' }\n\ninterface InterruptStore {\n  create(record: Omit<InterruptRecord, 'status' | 'resolvedAt'>): Promise<void>\n  commitBatch?(entries: ReadonlyArray<InterruptCommitEntry>): Promise<void>\n  resolve(interruptId: string, response?: unknown): Promise<void>\n  cancel(interruptId: string): Promise<void>\n  get(interruptId: string): Promise<InterruptRecord | null>\n  list(threadId: string): Promise<Array<InterruptRecord>>\n  listPending(threadId: string): Promise<Array<InterruptRecord>>\n  listByRun(runId: string): Promise<Array<InterruptRecord>>\n  listPendingByRun(runId: string): Promise<Array<InterruptRecord>>\n}\n```\n\n`commitBatch` is optional. Use one database transaction for all entries when\nyou implement it. The legacy `resolve` and `cancel` fallback is sequential and\nis not atomic.\n\nIf you implement `commitBatch`, reject the whole batch (throw, write nothing)\nwhen any entry has a duplicate `interruptId`, names an interrupt that does not\nexist, or names an interrupt that is not `'pending'`. `resolve` and `cancel`\nstay no-ops for a missing `interruptId`.\n\n`create` accepts a record without `status`/`resolvedAt` so every interrupt is\nborn `'pending'`; it is insert-if-absent, so a duplicate `create` never clobbers\nan already-resolved interrupt. The `list*` methods return records ordered by\n`requestedAt` ascending. An `interrupts` store requires a `runs` store when used\nwith chat persistence.\n\n## MetadataStore\n\n```ts\ninterface MetadataStore {\n  get(scope: string, key: string): Promise<unknown | null>\n  set(scope: string, key: string, value: unknown): Promise<void>\n  delete(scope: string, key: string): Promise<void>\n}\n```\n\nNamespaces and value schemas are application-owned, and `(scope, key)` is the\ncomposite identity. A stored `null` is indistinguishable from absence at the type\nlevel, so wrap a value you must persist as `null` (e.g. `{ value: null }`), or\nreject nullish values outright the way the SQLite store above does.\n\n`withPersistence` also provides this store through the core\n`MetadataCapability`. Middleware can use it for derived state without depending\non `@tanstack/ai-persistence`. For example, `withCompaction` stores validated\ncontext checkpoints here. Do not place the canonical transcript in metadata;\nthe `messages` store owns it.\n\n## GenerationRunStore\n\nThe generation counterpart to `RunStore`. Keyed by its own `runId`, with\n`threadId` the slot `findLatestForThread` looks runs up by.\n`withGenerationPersistence` requires this store, not `runs`.\n\nIts `status` uses the same vocabulary as a chat run's `RunStatus`, so one status\ncolumn and one set of checks cover both tables.\n\n```ts\nimport type { PersistedArtifactRef, TokenUsage } from '@tanstack/ai'\n\n// The same vocabulary as a chat run's `RunStatus`.\ntype GenerationRunStatus =\n  | 'running'\n  | 'interrupted'\n  | 'completed'\n  | 'failed'\n  | 'aborted'\n\ninterface GenerationRunRecord {\n  runId: string\n  threadId: string // the slot this run fills, hydrated by findLatestForThread\n  activity: string // 'image' | 'audio' | 'tts' | 'video' | 'transcription'\n  provider: string\n  model: string\n  status: GenerationRunStatus\n  startedAt: number // epoch ms\n  finishedAt?: number // epoch ms, set once the run reaches a terminal status\n  error?: { message: string; code?: string }\n  result?: unknown // terminal result metadata (ids, urls), never media bytes\n  artifacts?: Array<PersistedArtifactRef> // present with an artifacts + blobs backend\n  usage?: TokenUsage\n}\n\ninterface GenerationRunStore {\n  createOrResume(input: {\n    runId: string\n    activity: string\n    provider: string\n    model: string\n    startedAt: number\n    threadId: string\n    status?: GenerationRunStatus\n  }): Promise<GenerationRunRecord>\n  update(\n    runId: string,\n    patch: Partial<\n      Pick<\n        GenerationRunRecord,\n        'status' | 'finishedAt' | 'error' | 'result' | 'artifacts' | 'usage'\n      >\n    >,\n  ): Promise<void>\n  get(runId: string): Promise<GenerationRunRecord | null>\n  // The most recent run filed under a thread (greatest `startedAt`), or null.\n  // Required: it is the only query that hydrates a generation, so an adapter\n  // without it would be indistinguishable from one whose thread has no runs:\n  // `persistence: true` would silently restore nothing, forever.\n  findLatestForThread(threadId: string): Promise<GenerationRunRecord | null>\n}\n```\n\nImplement `createOrResume` idempotently: a second call for an existing `runId`\nreturns the stored record unchanged (`startedAt` / `activity` / `provider` /\n`model` / `threadId` are not mutated), which is what makes resuming a run safe.\n`update` against an unknown `runId` is a no-op.\n\n## ArtifactStore\n\nMetadata rows for persisted media. The bytes live in a `BlobStore`; this record\nholds the descriptive metadata and an optional `sourceUrl` for reference-only\nbackends. Provide it together with a `BlobStore` to keep generated bytes.\n\n```ts\ninterface ArtifactRecord {\n  artifactId: string\n  runId: string\n  threadId: string\n  blobKey?: string // where the bytes live; absent on pre-blobKey records\n  name: string\n  mimeType: string\n  size: number\n  sourceUrl?: string // where the bytes were fetched FROM (provenance)\n  createdAt: number // epoch ms\n}\n\ninterface ArtifactStore {\n  save: (record: ArtifactRecord) => Promise<void>\n  get: (artifactId: string) => Promise<ArtifactRecord | null>\n  list: (runId: string) => Promise<Array<ArtifactRecord>> // [] when the run has none\n  // Complete thread history, ordered by (createdAt, artifactId) ascending.\n  listForThread: (threadId: string) => Promise<Array<ArtifactRecord>>\n  delete: (artifactId: string) => Promise<void>\n  deleteForRun: (runId: string) => Promise<void>\n}\n```\n\n`list` and `listForThread` use `createdAt` first, then ordinal bytewise\n`artifactId` order. Compare UTF-8 bytes from left to right. Do not use locale\ncollation.\n\n## BlobStore\n\nA durable object/blob store for the bytes. `withGenerationPersistence` writes\neach generated file under the key `artifacts/<runId>/<artifactId>`.\n\n```ts\ntype BlobBody =\n  | ReadableStream<Uint8Array>\n  | ArrayBuffer\n  | ArrayBufferView\n  | string\n  | Blob\n\ninterface BlobRecord {\n  key: string\n  size?: number\n  etag?: string\n  contentType?: string\n  customMetadata?: Record<string, string>\n  createdAt?: number // epoch ms first written\n  updatedAt?: number // epoch ms last overwritten\n}\n\ninterface BlobObject extends BlobRecord {\n  arrayBuffer(): Promise<ArrayBuffer>\n  text(): Promise<string>\n  body?: ReadableStream<Uint8Array>\n  // The slice served, when a range was requested. Absent on a whole read.\n  range?: { offset: number; length: number }\n}\n\ninterface BlobListPage {\n  objects: Array<BlobRecord>\n  cursor?: string // present only when `truncated`\n  truncated?: boolean\n}\n\ninterface BlobPutOptions {\n  contentType?: string\n  customMetadata?: Record<string, string>\n  // Exact byte length of `body`, when the producer knows it. Advisory: use it\n  // to pick an upload strategy (single-shot vs multipart), never as a\n  // substitute for counting the bytes you actually store.\n  expectedLength?: number\n}\n\ninterface BlobRange {\n  offset: number // from the start of the object; must be inside it\n  length?: number // defaults to \"to the end\"; clamped when it overshoots\n}\n\ninterface BlobGetOptions {\n  range?: BlobRange\n}\n\ninterface BlobListOptions {\n  prefix?: string\n  cursor?: string\n  limit?: number\n}\n\ninterface BlobStore {\n  put(key: string, body: BlobBody, options?: BlobPutOptions): Promise<BlobRecord>\n  get(key: string, options?: BlobGetOptions): Promise<BlobObject | null>\n  head(key: string): Promise<BlobRecord | null>\n  delete(key: string): Promise<void>\n  list(options?: BlobListOptions): Promise<BlobListPage>\n}\n```\n\nThree contracts to hold for `list`:\n\n- `prefix` matches literally and case-sensitively. Escape SQL `LIKE`\n  metacharacters.\n- When `limit` is given and more keys match, return `truncated: true` with a\n  `cursor`. Passing that cursor back returns the strictly-following keys, so\n  paging visits every key exactly once.\n- `limit: 0` yields an empty, untruncated page.\n\nAnd one for `put`: the body can be a `ReadableStream` with **no declared\nlength**. That is how a URL-fetched artifact arrives whenever the origin does\nnot declare one it can be held to: a chunked reply, or a compressed one whose\n`content-length` describes the compressed bytes. (When the origin *does* declare\na usable length, the body reaches you exactly as `fetch` produced it, length\nintact, and `expectedLength` carries the same number.) Your store must drain a\nlength-less stream, not require a length up front. Backends that need a declared\nlength for a single-shot upload (Cloudflare R2 on workerd is one) can re-attach\n`expectedLength` when it is present and stream through a multipart upload when it\nis not; the `ai-persistence/build-cloudflare-artifact-store` skill ships that\nrecipe. The conformance testkit exercises the length-less case, so a store that\nonly handles byte bodies fails the suite.\n\nAnd one for `get`: honour `options.range` by returning **only that slice**.\n`size` keeps reporting the whole object, and the returned `range` reports what\nyou actually served. Together they are the `206` response a media player's\nseeking depends on. `resolveBlobRange(size, range)` does the clamping (a\n`length` past the end is legal and clamps; an `offset` past the end throws,\nbecause a serve route should have answered `416` from `record.size` first):\n\n```ts ignore\nimport { resolveBlobRange } from '@tanstack/ai-persistence'\n\nasync get(key: string, options?: BlobGetOptions) {\n  const row = await selectBlob(key)\n  if (!row) return null\n  if (!options?.range) return blobObject(row, row.body)\n  const served = resolveBlobRange(row.size, options.range)\n  // Slice at the storage layer, not after loading the whole object.\n  const bytes = await selectBlobSlice(key, served.offset, served.length)\n  return blobObject(row, bytes, served)\n}\n```\n\nThis is not optional for a store that holds bytes, and the conformance testkit\nasserts it. Ignoring `range` and returning the whole file is what makes\n`<video>` seeking (and Safari playback at all) fail, and it silently sends the\nentire artifact for every seek. A reference-only backend that stores no bytes\nskips `blobs` altogether instead.\n\n## How the records relate\n\nThe thread is not a table of its own: it exists as the `thread_id` the other records\nhang off. `metadata` stands apart, keyed by `(namespace, key)`. Note the asymmetry on\nthe generation side, where a run is keyed by its own `run_id` first and its\n`thread_id` names the slot the run fills, which is what `findLatestForThread`\nhydrates by.\n\n```mermaid\nerDiagram\n    MESSAGES ||--o{ RUN : \"thread_id, a thread has many runs\"\n    RUN ||--o{ INTERRUPT : \"run_id, a run may pause on interrupts\"\n    MESSAGES ||..o{ GENERATION_RUN : \"thread_id, the slot a run fills\"\n    GENERATION_RUN ||--o{ ARTIFACT : \"run_id, a run produces artifacts\"\n    ARTIFACT ||--|| BLOB : \"blob_key, the bytes\"\n\n    MESSAGES {\n        string thread_id PK\n        json messages_json \"full transcript, overwritten on save\"\n    }\n    RUN {\n        string run_id PK\n        string thread_id\n        string status \"running | completed | failed | interrupted\"\n        int started_at\n        int finished_at\n    }\n    INTERRUPT {\n        string interrupt_id PK\n        string run_id\n        string thread_id\n        string status \"pending | resolved | cancelled\"\n        int requested_at\n    }\n    GENERATION_RUN {\n        string run_id PK\n        string thread_id \"the slot this run fills\"\n        string activity \"image | audio | tts | video | transcription\"\n        string status \"running | completed | failed | interrupted\"\n    }\n    ARTIFACT {\n        string artifact_id PK\n        string run_id\n        string blob_key \"where the bytes live\"\n        string mime_type\n        int size\n    }\n    BLOB {\n        string key PK\n        blob bytes\n    }\n```\n\n## Typing an adapter\n\nEach store has a `define*Store` helper (`defineMessageStore`, `defineRunStore`,\n`defineInterruptStore`, `defineMetadataStore`, `defineGenerationRunStore`,\n`defineArtifactStore`, `defineBlobStore`). They compose into `defineAIPersistence`,\nwhich tracks exact presence: the stores you passed autocomplete on\n`persistence.stores`, and reading one you did not pass is a compile error.\n\nThose seven keys are the only ones `stores` accepts. Anything else throws\n`Unknown AIPersistence store key` at construction.\n\nTo annotate the value instead, use a named shape:\n\n- `ChatPersistence`: all four chat stores.\n- `ChatTranscriptPersistence`: the `messages` floor.\n- `AIPersistence`: the all-optional bag. `withPersistence` rejects it, because\n  `stores.messages` is possibly `undefined`.\n\n## Where to go next\n\n- [Build a chat adapter](./build-your-own-chat-adapter): these contracts implemented against SQLite.\n- [Build a generation adapter](./build-your-own-generation-adapter): the generation half.\n- [Build your own adapter](./build-your-own-adapter#verify-with-the-conformance-suite): check an implementation against the conformance suite.\n","originalRawMarkdown":"---\ntitle: Store Reference (Advanced)\nid: store-reference\n---\n\nThese are the public contracts from `@tanstack/ai-persistence`. Implement only the\nstores you need. Middleware turns behavior on from whichever stores are present, so\nthere is no separate enable list.\n\n| Store | Purpose | Used by |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| `messages` | Authoritative model-message history per thread. | `withPersistence`, required |\n| `runs` | Run status, timing, errors, usage. | `withPersistence` |\n| `interrupts` | Pending, resolved or cancelled human waits. Needs `runs`. | `withPersistence` |\n| `metadata` | App and integration key/value state. | `withPersistence` |\n| `generationRuns` | Generation run status and result metadata, keyed by its own `runId`. | `withGenerationPersistence`, required |\n| `artifacts` | File metadata. Needs `blobs`. | `withGenerationPersistence`, portable snapshots |\n| `blobs` | File bytes. Needs `artifacts`. | `withGenerationPersistence`, portable snapshots |\n\nNamed groupings of the chat stores (`ChatTranscriptStores`, `ChatPersistenceStores`,\n`ChatWithInterruptsStores`) are covered in [Controls](./controls).\n\n`runs` is typed against `RunRecord` and `RunStore` from `@tanstack/ai`, not from this\npackage. One definition is what lets chat persistence and a sandbox run driver key on\nthe same record instead of disagreeing about one run.\n\n## MessageStore\n\n```ts\nimport type { ModelMessage } from '@tanstack/ai'\n\ntype MessagePage =\n  | {\n      messages: Array<ModelMessage>\n      truncated: false\n      cursor?: never\n    }\n  | {\n      messages: Array<ModelMessage>\n      truncated: true\n      cursor: string\n    }\n\ninterface MessageStore {\n  loadThread(\n    threadId: string,\n    options?: { limit?: number; before?: string },\n  ): Promise<Array<ModelMessage> | MessagePage>\n  saveThread(threadId: string, messages: Array<ModelMessage>): Promise<void>\n}\n```\n\n- `loadThread` with only `threadId` (middleware, `onStart`, `onFinish`) returns\n  the full array. Never a `MessagePage`.\n- `limit` and `before` are a paging hint for hydrate. You can ignore them and\n  return the full array. `reconstructChat` then slices after UI conversion.\n- To page in the database, return a `MessagePage`. `truncated: true` requires a\n  `cursor`. `truncated` and `cursor` use the same words as `BlobStore.list`.\n- If you ignore `before` and return the newest array again, `reconstructChat`\n  loads the full thread and slices.\n- `saveThread` receives the full merged list. It is a replace, not an append.\n  Merge by id is `withPersistence`, not this store.\n\n`loadThread` returns `[]` (never `null`) for a thread that was never saved.\n\n## RunStore\n\n`RunStore` and `RunRecord` come from `@tanstack/ai`; `@tanstack/ai-persistence`\nre-exports both, which is why the samples on this page import them from either\npackage. `RunError` is exported from `@tanstack/ai` only.\n\n```ts\nimport type { TokenUsage } from '@tanstack/ai'\n\ntype TerminalRunStatus = 'completed' | 'failed' | 'aborted'\ntype RunStatus = 'running' | 'interrupted' | TerminalRunStatus\n\n// Why a run failed. `message` is the provider's prose, which changes between\n// model versions and cannot be branched on; `code` is the stable\n// classification a consumer switches over to retry, escalate, or show a\n// specific UI. Providers do not always supply one, so `code` is optional.\ninterface RunError {\n  message: string\n  code?: string\n}\n\ninterface RunRecord {\n  runId: string\n  threadId: string\n  // Present on a subagent child run. Absent on the parent chat run.\n  parentRunId?: string\n  subagentRunId?: string\n  name?: string\n  status: RunStatus\n  startedAt: number // epoch ms\n  finishedAt?: number // epoch ms, set once the run reaches a terminal status\n  error?: RunError\n  usage?: TokenUsage // reported usage accumulated for this runId\n  // ---------------------------------------------------------------------------\n  // DURABLE SANDBOXED RUNS ONLY. A chat app never writes these four and nothing\n  // in `@tanstack/ai-persistence` reads them. Leave the columns out until you\n  // wire `withSandbox(sandbox, { runs, durability })`, and see\n  // [Build a Sandbox Adapter](./build-a-sandbox-adapter#the-four-run-fields)\n  // for what each one does and how to prove them.\n  // ---------------------------------------------------------------------------\n  sandboxKey?: string // which sandbox this run is bound to\n  detachedSince?: number // epoch ms since the last viewer left\n  cancelRequested?: boolean // an out-of-band cancel was recorded\n  driverEpoch?: number // fencing token, bumped by each host that claims the run\n}\n\ninterface RunStore {\n  // Required: insert-if-absent. An existing runId returns the stored record\n  // unchanged, so resuming a run never resets its startedAt or status.\n  createOrResume(input: {\n    runId: string\n    threadId: string\n    status?: RunStatus\n    startedAt: number\n    // Copied on the first insert only. A later call leaves them unchanged.\n    parentRunId?: string\n    subagentRunId?: string\n    name?: string\n  }): Promise<RunRecord>\n  // Required: patching an unknown runId is a no-op, not an error.\n  update(\n    runId: string,\n    patch: Partial<\n      Pick<\n        RunRecord,\n        | 'status'\n        | 'finishedAt'\n        | 'error'\n        | 'usage'\n        | 'sandboxKey'\n        | 'detachedSince'\n        | 'cancelRequested'\n        | 'driverEpoch'\n      >\n    >,\n  ): Promise<void>\n  // Required.\n  get(runId: string): Promise<RunRecord | null>\n  // Required. The most recent 'running' run for a thread (greatest\n  // `startedAt` wins), or null when the thread is idle. `reconstructChat`\n  // calls it to report `activeRun`, which is how a hydrating client tails a\n  // run that is still generating.\n  findActiveRun(threadId: string): Promise<RunRecord | null>\n  // Optional. Every run for a thread, ascending by startedAt. Only needed to\n  // render a thread's past agent activity.\n  listByThread?(threadId: string): Promise<Array<RunRecord>>\n  // Optional. Child runs for one parent, oldest startedAt first.\n  // reconstructChat uses this to put subagent cards back on the parent message.\n  listByParentRun?(parentRunId: string): Promise<Array<RunRecord>>\n  // Optional. Runs where status is 'running' and detachedSince <= now - ttlMs\n  // (inclusive). This is the query `reapDetachedRuns` (@tanstack/ai-sandbox)\n  // runs to find abandoned runs; scheduling that sweep is the app's job.\n  listReclaimable?(opts: {\n    now: number\n    ttlMs: number\n  }): Promise<Array<RunRecord>>\n}\n```\n\n`withPersistence` sums reported numeric usage fields across provider calls for\nthe same `runId`. The opaque `providerUsageDetails` field retains the latest\nreported bag. Known usage is persisted when the run interrupts or reaches a\nterminal status.\n\n`createOrResume`, `update`, `get`, and `findActiveRun` are the floor: a backend\nthat implements those four is a valid `RunStore`. Three contracts to hold:\n\n- `createOrResume` must be idempotent. A second call for an existing `runId`\n  returns the complete stored record unchanged, including `usage`. This makes\n  resuming a run safe and lets usage continue accumulating. Retries may repeat\n  the same run id.\n- `update` against an unknown `runId` is a no-op.\n- `findActiveRun` must do real work. Stub it to `null` and `reconstructChat`\n  always reports `activeRun: null`, so a client that reloads (or switches back\n  to) a still-generating thread restores the transcript but never resumes the\n  live reply. Nothing detects it either, because `null` is also the right answer\n  for an idle thread. It was optional for exactly one release cycle and cost\n  precisely that, which is why it is required now.\n\n`listByThread`, `listByParentRun`, and `listReclaimable` are optional.\nConsumers feature-detect each one. After you declare an omission of\n`listByThread` or `listReclaimable` in `skipMethods`, the conformance suite\nskips that method. Implement the ones\nyour app needs:\n\n- Skip `listByThread` and a reload loses the cards of children that a tool call\n  started. `reconstructChat` calls it to find their parent runs.\n- Skip `listByParentRun` and a reload shows the saved child text. The subagent\n  cards stay absent. `reconstructChat` calls this method to build the cards.\n  The conformance suite skips the subagent checks when this method is absent,\n  so it needs no `skipMethods` entry.\n- Skip `listReclaimable` and the store cannot be reaped: `reapDetachedRuns`\n  feature-detects it, logs one line, and sweeps nothing, so detached runs are\n  never finalized and their sandboxes never reclaimed. `detachedSince` *is*\n  populated for you by `withSandbox`'s detach path, and note that the reaper is a\n  function the application schedules, so implementing this method is necessary\n  but not by itself sufficient. See\n  [Takeover & Detached Runs](../sandbox/takeover#configuration) for the option,\n  and [Reaping & Retention](../sandbox/reaping) for the sweep itself and the\n  schedules that drive it.\n\nThe four durable-run fields are a sandbox concern, so they are documented and\nproven on the sandbox side:\n[Build a Sandbox Adapter](./build-a-sandbox-adapter#the-four-run-fields).\n`runPersistenceConformance` does not assert them, and a chat-only backend can leave\nthe columns out entirely. If you do wire durable sandboxed runs, run\n`runDurableRunFieldsConformance` from `@tanstack/ai-sandbox/testkit` against the same\n`runs` store: each field breaks one mechanism silently when dropped, which is why it\ngets its own suite rather than a paragraph of warnings.\n\nTwo helpers travel with the contract, and both exist so a caller does not have\nto reach for a cast:\n\n- Deciding \"can this run still emit events?\" from a bare `RunStatus` string\n  leaves you holding the wide union afterwards. `isTerminalRunStatus` is a type\n  predicate, so inside the guard the status is a `TerminalRunStatus` and can be\n  passed wherever one is required.\n\n  ```ts\n  import { isTerminalRunStatus } from '@tanstack/ai-persistence'\n  import type { RunRecord, TerminalRunStatus } from '@tanstack/ai-persistence'\n\n  function finalStatus(run: RunRecord): TerminalRunStatus | null {\n    return isTerminalRunStatus(run.status) ? run.status : null\n  }\n  ```\n\n- An optional method you did implement should not need a `?.` from inside your\n  own code. `defineRunStore` is generic over the object you pass, so the result\n  keeps that object's exact shape: `createRunStore(db).findActiveRun(threadId)`\n  is a direct call on the store built in the\n  [chat walkthrough](./build-your-own-chat-adapter), while a consumer holding a\n  bare `RunStore` still feature-detects the same method.\n\nCapability tiers belong at the STORE level, not the method level. A backend that\ngenuinely has no run lifecycle should declare `ChatTranscriptStores` and omit\n`runs` entirely rather than supply a `RunStore` with a stubbed method: an absent\nstore is caught by the type system, an incomplete one fails silently at runtime.\n\n`createOrResume` copies `parentRunId`, `subagentRunId`, and `name` on the first\ninsert. A second call for the same `runId` leaves those fields unchanged. If\nthe caller omits a field, omit that field on the stored record. Do not write\n`''` for a field the caller did not pass.\n\nThe reference implementation, `MemoryRunStore` in\n`packages/ai-persistence/src/memory.ts`, implements all seven run methods. The\n`examples/ts-react-chat` SQLite adapter (`src/lib/sqlite-persistence.ts`)\nimplements all seven run methods.\n\n## InterruptStore\n\n```ts\ninterface InterruptRecord {\n  interruptId: string\n  runId: string\n  threadId: string\n  status: 'pending' | 'resolved' | 'cancelled'\n  requestedAt: number // epoch ms\n  resolvedAt?: number // epoch ms, set once resolved or cancelled\n  payload: Record<string, unknown>\n  response?: unknown\n}\n\ntype InterruptCommitEntry =\n  | { interruptId: string; status: 'resolved'; response?: unknown }\n  | { interruptId: string; status: 'cancelled' }\n\ninterface InterruptStore {\n  create(record: Omit<InterruptRecord, 'status' | 'resolvedAt'>): Promise<void>\n  commitBatch?(entries: ReadonlyArray<InterruptCommitEntry>): Promise<void>\n  resolve(interruptId: string, response?: unknown): Promise<void>\n  cancel(interruptId: string): Promise<void>\n  get(interruptId: string): Promise<InterruptRecord | null>\n  list(threadId: string): Promise<Array<InterruptRecord>>\n  listPending(threadId: string): Promise<Array<InterruptRecord>>\n  listByRun(runId: string): Promise<Array<InterruptRecord>>\n  listPendingByRun(runId: string): Promise<Array<InterruptRecord>>\n}\n```\n\n`commitBatch` is optional. Use one database transaction for all entries when\nyou implement it. The legacy `resolve` and `cancel` fallback is sequential and\nis not atomic.\n\nIf you implement `commitBatch`, reject the whole batch (throw, write nothing)\nwhen any entry has a duplicate `interruptId`, names an interrupt that does not\nexist, or names an interrupt that is not `'pending'`. `resolve` and `cancel`\nstay no-ops for a missing `interruptId`.\n\n`create` accepts a record without `status`/`resolvedAt` so every interrupt is\nborn `'pending'`; it is insert-if-absent, so a duplicate `create` never clobbers\nan already-resolved interrupt. The `list*` methods return records ordered by\n`requestedAt` ascending. An `interrupts` store requires a `runs` store when used\nwith chat persistence.\n\n## MetadataStore\n\n```ts\ninterface MetadataStore {\n  get(scope: string, key: string): Promise<unknown | null>\n  set(scope: string, key: string, value: unknown): Promise<void>\n  delete(scope: string, key: string): Promise<void>\n}\n```\n\nNamespaces and value schemas are application-owned, and `(scope, key)` is the\ncomposite identity. A stored `null` is indistinguishable from absence at the type\nlevel, so wrap a value you must persist as `null` (e.g. `{ value: null }`), or\nreject nullish values outright the way the SQLite store above does.\n\n`withPersistence` also provides this store through the core\n`MetadataCapability`. Middleware can use it for derived state without depending\non `@tanstack/ai-persistence`. For example, `withCompaction` stores validated\ncontext checkpoints here. Do not place the canonical transcript in metadata;\nthe `messages` store owns it.\n\n## GenerationRunStore\n\nThe generation counterpart to `RunStore`. Keyed by its own `runId`, with\n`threadId` the slot `findLatestForThread` looks runs up by.\n`withGenerationPersistence` requires this store, not `runs`.\n\nIts `status` uses the same vocabulary as a chat run's `RunStatus`, so one status\ncolumn and one set of checks cover both tables.\n\n```ts\nimport type { PersistedArtifactRef, TokenUsage } from '@tanstack/ai'\n\n// The same vocabulary as a chat run's `RunStatus`.\ntype GenerationRunStatus =\n  | 'running'\n  | 'interrupted'\n  | 'completed'\n  | 'failed'\n  | 'aborted'\n\ninterface GenerationRunRecord {\n  runId: string\n  threadId: string // the slot this run fills, hydrated by findLatestForThread\n  activity: string // 'image' | 'audio' | 'tts' | 'video' | 'transcription'\n  provider: string\n  model: string\n  status: GenerationRunStatus\n  startedAt: number // epoch ms\n  finishedAt?: number // epoch ms, set once the run reaches a terminal status\n  error?: { message: string; code?: string }\n  result?: unknown // terminal result metadata (ids, urls), never media bytes\n  artifacts?: Array<PersistedArtifactRef> // present with an artifacts + blobs backend\n  usage?: TokenUsage\n}\n\ninterface GenerationRunStore {\n  createOrResume(input: {\n    runId: string\n    activity: string\n    provider: string\n    model: string\n    startedAt: number\n    threadId: string\n    status?: GenerationRunStatus\n  }): Promise<GenerationRunRecord>\n  update(\n    runId: string,\n    patch: Partial<\n      Pick<\n        GenerationRunRecord,\n        'status' | 'finishedAt' | 'error' | 'result' | 'artifacts' | 'usage'\n      >\n    >,\n  ): Promise<void>\n  get(runId: string): Promise<GenerationRunRecord | null>\n  // The most recent run filed under a thread (greatest `startedAt`), or null.\n  // Required: it is the only query that hydrates a generation, so an adapter\n  // without it would be indistinguishable from one whose thread has no runs:\n  // `persistence: true` would silently restore nothing, forever.\n  findLatestForThread(threadId: string): Promise<GenerationRunRecord | null>\n}\n```\n\nImplement `createOrResume` idempotently: a second call for an existing `runId`\nreturns the stored record unchanged (`startedAt` / `activity` / `provider` /\n`model` / `threadId` are not mutated), which is what makes resuming a run safe.\n`update` against an unknown `runId` is a no-op.\n\n## ArtifactStore\n\nMetadata rows for persisted media. The bytes live in a `BlobStore`; this record\nholds the descriptive metadata and an optional `sourceUrl` for reference-only\nbackends. Provide it together with a `BlobStore` to keep generated bytes.\n\n```ts\ninterface ArtifactRecord {\n  artifactId: string\n  runId: string\n  threadId: string\n  blobKey?: string // where the bytes live; absent on pre-blobKey records\n  name: string\n  mimeType: string\n  size: number\n  sourceUrl?: string // where the bytes were fetched FROM (provenance)\n  createdAt: number // epoch ms\n}\n\ninterface ArtifactStore {\n  save: (record: ArtifactRecord) => Promise<void>\n  get: (artifactId: string) => Promise<ArtifactRecord | null>\n  list: (runId: string) => Promise<Array<ArtifactRecord>> // [] when the run has none\n  // Complete thread history, ordered by (createdAt, artifactId) ascending.\n  listForThread: (threadId: string) => Promise<Array<ArtifactRecord>>\n  delete: (artifactId: string) => Promise<void>\n  deleteForRun: (runId: string) => Promise<void>\n}\n```\n\n`list` and `listForThread` use `createdAt` first, then ordinal bytewise\n`artifactId` order. Compare UTF-8 bytes from left to right. Do not use locale\ncollation.\n\n## BlobStore\n\nA durable object/blob store for the bytes. `withGenerationPersistence` writes\neach generated file under the key `artifacts/<runId>/<artifactId>`.\n\n```ts\ntype BlobBody =\n  | ReadableStream<Uint8Array>\n  | ArrayBuffer\n  | ArrayBufferView\n  | string\n  | Blob\n\ninterface BlobRecord {\n  key: string\n  size?: number\n  etag?: string\n  contentType?: string\n  customMetadata?: Record<string, string>\n  createdAt?: number // epoch ms first written\n  updatedAt?: number // epoch ms last overwritten\n}\n\ninterface BlobObject extends BlobRecord {\n  arrayBuffer(): Promise<ArrayBuffer>\n  text(): Promise<string>\n  body?: ReadableStream<Uint8Array>\n  // The slice served, when a range was requested. Absent on a whole read.\n  range?: { offset: number; length: number }\n}\n\ninterface BlobListPage {\n  objects: Array<BlobRecord>\n  cursor?: string // present only when `truncated`\n  truncated?: boolean\n}\n\ninterface BlobPutOptions {\n  contentType?: string\n  customMetadata?: Record<string, string>\n  // Exact byte length of `body`, when the producer knows it. Advisory: use it\n  // to pick an upload strategy (single-shot vs multipart), never as a\n  // substitute for counting the bytes you actually store.\n  expectedLength?: number\n}\n\ninterface BlobRange {\n  offset: number // from the start of the object; must be inside it\n  length?: number // defaults to \"to the end\"; clamped when it overshoots\n}\n\ninterface BlobGetOptions {\n  range?: BlobRange\n}\n\ninterface BlobListOptions {\n  prefix?: string\n  cursor?: string\n  limit?: number\n}\n\ninterface BlobStore {\n  put(key: string, body: BlobBody, options?: BlobPutOptions): Promise<BlobRecord>\n  get(key: string, options?: BlobGetOptions): Promise<BlobObject | null>\n  head(key: string): Promise<BlobRecord | null>\n  delete(key: string): Promise<void>\n  list(options?: BlobListOptions): Promise<BlobListPage>\n}\n```\n\nThree contracts to hold for `list`:\n\n- `prefix` matches literally and case-sensitively. Escape SQL `LIKE`\n  metacharacters.\n- When `limit` is given and more keys match, return `truncated: true` with a\n  `cursor`. Passing that cursor back returns the strictly-following keys, so\n  paging visits every key exactly once.\n- `limit: 0` yields an empty, untruncated page.\n\nAnd one for `put`: the body can be a `ReadableStream` with **no declared\nlength**. That is how a URL-fetched artifact arrives whenever the origin does\nnot declare one it can be held to: a chunked reply, or a compressed one whose\n`content-length` describes the compressed bytes. (When the origin *does* declare\na usable length, the body reaches you exactly as `fetch` produced it, length\nintact, and `expectedLength` carries the same number.) Your store must drain a\nlength-less stream, not require a length up front. Backends that need a declared\nlength for a single-shot upload (Cloudflare R2 on workerd is one) can re-attach\n`expectedLength` when it is present and stream through a multipart upload when it\nis not; the `ai-persistence/build-cloudflare-artifact-store` skill ships that\nrecipe. The conformance testkit exercises the length-less case, so a store that\nonly handles byte bodies fails the suite.\n\nAnd one for `get`: honour `options.range` by returning **only that slice**.\n`size` keeps reporting the whole object, and the returned `range` reports what\nyou actually served. Together they are the `206` response a media player's\nseeking depends on. `resolveBlobRange(size, range)` does the clamping (a\n`length` past the end is legal and clamps; an `offset` past the end throws,\nbecause a serve route should have answered `416` from `record.size` first):\n\n```ts ignore\nimport { resolveBlobRange } from '@tanstack/ai-persistence'\n\nasync get(key: string, options?: BlobGetOptions) {\n  const row = await selectBlob(key)\n  if (!row) return null\n  if (!options?.range) return blobObject(row, row.body)\n  const served = resolveBlobRange(row.size, options.range)\n  // Slice at the storage layer, not after loading the whole object.\n  const bytes = await selectBlobSlice(key, served.offset, served.length)\n  return blobObject(row, bytes, served)\n}\n```\n\nThis is not optional for a store that holds bytes, and the conformance testkit\nasserts it. Ignoring `range` and returning the whole file is what makes\n`<video>` seeking (and Safari playback at all) fail, and it silently sends the\nentire artifact for every seek. A reference-only backend that stores no bytes\nskips `blobs` altogether instead.\n\n## How the records relate\n\nThe thread is not a table of its own: it exists as the `thread_id` the other records\nhang off. `metadata` stands apart, keyed by `(namespace, key)`. Note the asymmetry on\nthe generation side, where a run is keyed by its own `run_id` first and its\n`thread_id` names the slot the run fills, which is what `findLatestForThread`\nhydrates by.\n\n```mermaid\nerDiagram\n    MESSAGES ||--o{ RUN : \"thread_id, a thread has many runs\"\n    RUN ||--o{ INTERRUPT : \"run_id, a run may pause on interrupts\"\n    MESSAGES ||..o{ GENERATION_RUN : \"thread_id, the slot a run fills\"\n    GENERATION_RUN ||--o{ ARTIFACT : \"run_id, a run produces artifacts\"\n    ARTIFACT ||--|| BLOB : \"blob_key, the bytes\"\n\n    MESSAGES {\n        string thread_id PK\n        json messages_json \"full transcript, overwritten on save\"\n    }\n    RUN {\n        string run_id PK\n        string thread_id\n        string status \"running | completed | failed | interrupted\"\n        int started_at\n        int finished_at\n    }\n    INTERRUPT {\n        string interrupt_id PK\n        string run_id\n        string thread_id\n        string status \"pending | resolved | cancelled\"\n        int requested_at\n    }\n    GENERATION_RUN {\n        string run_id PK\n        string thread_id \"the slot this run fills\"\n        string activity \"image | audio | tts | video | transcription\"\n        string status \"running | completed | failed | interrupted\"\n    }\n    ARTIFACT {\n        string artifact_id PK\n        string run_id\n        string blob_key \"where the bytes live\"\n        string mime_type\n        int size\n    }\n    BLOB {\n        string key PK\n        blob bytes\n    }\n```\n\n## Typing an adapter\n\nEach store has a `define*Store` helper (`defineMessageStore`, `defineRunStore`,\n`defineInterruptStore`, `defineMetadataStore`, `defineGenerationRunStore`,\n`defineArtifactStore`, `defineBlobStore`). They compose into `defineAIPersistence`,\nwhich tracks exact presence: the stores you passed autocomplete on\n`persistence.stores`, and reading one you did not pass is a compile error.\n\nThose seven keys are the only ones `stores` accepts. Anything else throws\n`Unknown AIPersistence store key` at construction.\n\nTo annotate the value instead, use a named shape:\n\n- `ChatPersistence`: all four chat stores.\n- `ChatTranscriptPersistence`: the `messages` floor.\n- `AIPersistence`: the all-optional bag. `withPersistence` rejects it, because\n  `stores.messages` is possibly `undefined`.\n\n## Where to go next\n\n- [Build a chat adapter](./build-your-own-chat-adapter): these contracts implemented against SQLite.\n- [Build a generation adapter](./build-your-own-generation-adapter): the generation half.\n- [Build your own adapter](./build-your-own-adapter#verify-with-the-conformance-suite): check an implementation against the conformance suite.\n"}
